                <pb facs="0683" n="593" sameAs="frus:frus1934v03/pg593"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.825/19: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-10-15T11:00:00+08:00">October 15, 1931—11
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received October 15—5:20 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>776. Department’s 352, September 29, noon. The Consulate General at Shanghai
                    replies as follows: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“October 14, 1 p.m. It would be appreciated and of considerable
                            assistance to have Department’s views now with specific citations in
                            regard to paragraph 1 since it is learned from informal conversation
                            that there is by no means unanimity of opinion among my colleagues
                            regarding the applicability of annex 17 of Protocol of 1901 and
                            subsequent agreements in regard to Whangpoo Conservancy.</p>
                        <p>Second paragraph instruction is being studied jointly with shipping
                            concerns and when concluded will be combined with paragraph 3 of
                            instruction and report submitted.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <closer>For the Minister:<lb/><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                        >Engert</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
